%[text] %[text:anchor:T_6C25F7E4] # Color Segmentation by App
%[text] In this exercise,  we segment an image of strawberries using the ColorThresholder and Image Segmenter Apps
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_37B586C1] ## Load Image
%[text] - Right-Click and "Clear all Output" \
clearvars
close all

mmSetUnitDataFolder(2) % change folder to unit2 %[output:62af071f]
% name = 'strawberries.png'; % create a structure p (p for pic or photo)
p.rgb = imread('strawberries.png'); % add image to structure
imshow(p.rgb) %[output:5bacfc8a]
%[text] - how can we segment out the strawberries? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A139EAD3] ## Review Channel Histograms 
%[text] Here we use **mmShowHist** to display the three channels and their histograms
mmShowHist(p.rgb)
%[text] - notice that there is nice contrast between strawberries and the background in the green and red channels
%[text] - In the blue channel, it's harder to see the strawberries \
%%
%[text] ### Segmenting the Red Channel 
%[text] Strawberries are red, so this should be a no-brainer—just segment the red channel right?
p.red = p.rgb(:,:,1); % segment out the red channel
p.rMask = imbinarize(p.red); % create mask and add to p structure
figure;
imshow(p.rMask) % display mask
%[text] - Hmm, not great
%[text] - Lot's of noise \
%[text] #### 
%%
%[text] #### Burn Mask onto original Image to better review mask
%[text] Here we use the function **imoverlay** to create a new image where the mask is shown in yellow. This is known as "burning" the mask into the image. It makes it a little easier to see the mask on the original image
figure
burnedImg = imoverlay(p.rgb,p.rMask);
imshow(burnedImg); title('base mask')
%[text] - yeah, not great — we're missing some of the strawberries \
%%
%[text] The course function `mmShowBurnImage` comprise the `imoverlay` and `imsho`w steps into one function call, so its more similar to imshowpair (and easier to use).
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb, p.rMask)
%[text] - so, we'll be using this a lot, but in a pinch (e.g.  MATLAB grader), you can just use `imoverlay` and `imshow`. \
%%
%[text] ### Segmenting the Green channel
%[text] Maybe the green channel works better.  
figure;
p.green = p.rgb(:,:,2);
mmShowHist(p.green)
%[text] - notice in the green channel, the strawberries are darker than the background, so we should binarize the complement \
%%
%[text] #### Mask the green channel
p.greenc = imcomplement(p.green); % create complement
p.gMask = imbinarize(p.greenc); % create mask of complement
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,p.gMask) % course function to burn mask on image
%[text] - and that's even worse \
%%
%[text] #### Manually thresholding
%[text] Maybe the imbinarize default cut-off is too low. Let's set our own cut-off:
p.gMask150 = p.greenc > 190; % mask intensities greater than 190
figure
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,p.gMask150)
%[text] - still not great \
%%
%[text] ## Prototyping Color Segmentation
%[text] Ok, so, we are going to need something a little more sophisticated than just segmenting the red or green Channel. The colorThresholder App can help us prototype our approach
colorThresholder(p.rgb)
%[text] - Use the lasso tool to capture the strawberries
%[text] - Notice that we are using all three channels to segment the strawberry
%[text] - Manually Move the Green and red Sliders to clear up some of the noise
%[text] - Export the mask as maskRGB \
%[text] ![](text:image:6daf)
%%
%[text] ### RGB Result
figure
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,maskRGB)
%[text] - works, but it would need some serious morphological clean-up \
%%
%[text] ### HSV Result
%[text] Repeat the process with the HSV Colorspace
%[text] 1. Go back to the colorThresholder window
%[text] 2. Click on the "New Color Space" Window
%[text] 3. Select HSV
%[text] 4. Repeat the steps above: Notice that there is a Hue channel that allows us to specifically choose Hues (colors)
%[text] 5. Export the mask as `maskHSV`. \
figure
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,maskHSV)
%[text] - much better (and easier to deal with!) \
%%
%[text] ### LAB Result
%[text] Repeat the process with the LAB Colorspace
%[text] 1. Go back to the colorThresholder window
%[text] 2. Click on the "New Color Space" Window
%[text] 3. Select LAB
%[text] 4. Compare the Channels. a - green to red, b - blue to yellow.
%[text] 5. Repeat the steps above by circling the strawberries.
%[text] 6. Export the mask as `maskLAB`. \
figure
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,maskLAB)
%[text] - a little noisier than HSV, but easier than RGB \
%%
%[text] ### Roll your own Segmentation Function
%[text] If you are going to threshold the image or similar images, repeatedly, it is easier to create a function instead of manually thresholding each image. You can create a function from colorThresholder using the export menu. The colorThresholder function creates nearly the exact same code for each color model. Basically, it captures the Slider settings from the histograms of each channel and then creates a mask from these settings using logical operations. So, the only lines in the code that change are the lines where the image is converted to the chosen color space and where the thresholds are defined for each channel based. Below, I have created the outline of a function that will allow you to switch between the different color models to create a mask
%[text] - For each function that you create using colorThresholder, copy the code from:  `I = ...` TO  SliderBW `= ...`.
%[text] - Paste the code into the appropriate case of the SWITCH CASE statement below or create a new case as needed
%[text] - We are going to ignore the maskedImage creation — that's like a poor man's Burned Mask Image, we have better options.  \
%[text] NOTE: Don't worry if you mess up, there is a back up copy of the completed function at the bottom of this live script.
%[text] 
mask = mySegmentStrawberries(p.rgb,'rgb'); % call your function as follows
figure; imshow(mask)

function [BW] = mySegmentStrawberries(RGB,colorModel)
% Converts Image to selected color model and then creates a mask based on
% the histogram slider settings for each channel
sliderBW = false(height(RGB),width(RGB)); %preallocate mask

switch colorModel
    case 'rgb'
       
    otherwise
        disp('color model not found')
        return
end


BW = sliderBW; % copy mask
BW = bwareaopen(BW,500); % remove noise
BW = imfill(BW,'holes');

end

%%
%[text] ### Review Mask
%[text] Now that we have a function, we can quickly review and compare our masks. We can even include some morphological operations
figure
mmTightTiledLayout % horizontal, no spacing

for n=["rgb" "hsv" "lab"]
    mask = SegmentStrawberries(rgb,n); % threshold function from bottom of this live script
    mask = bwareaopen(mask,500); % remove noise
    nexttile
    mmShowBurnImage(rgb,mask)
    title(n)
end
%%
%[text] ### Comparing Masks
%[text] There are a couple of calculations often used to compare masks: Jaccard Similarity Coefficient and the Dice calculation. 
%[text] - The **Jaccard index** is defined as taking the intersection size divided by the union size (MASK AND divided by MASK OR). This is also called **intersection over union** (**IoU**).
%[text] - The dice coefficient is slightly different \
%[text] ![](text:image:2209)
%[text] Both functions spit out a number between 0 and 1, with the higher value meaning closer similarity
%[text] These functions are very easy to use. You just input the two masks that you want to compare
jaccard(maskRGB,maskLAB) 
dice(maskRGB, maskHSV)
%[text] - Jaccard is a little more restrictive \
%%
%[text] ### Create a compare function
%[text] To compare the masks with both the jaccard and dice index, it would be easier to create a function with a FOR LOOP to handle all the heavily lifting. But to compare a two different masks on each iteration of the loop, we need to come up with a way to create a series of permutations, as follows: nchoosek returns all possible combinations of the elements of vector `v` taken `k` at a time
%[text] YOU TRY: Change the vector to `1:4`
nchoosek(1:3,2) % pick two from the vector 1:3

function t = compareMasks(rgb)
% Compare Masks and display overlays

colorModels = ["rgb" "lab" "hsv"]'; % a list of color modes
num = numel(colorModels);
V = nchoosek(1:num,2); % create all combinations
t = table(strings(num,1), zeros(num,1),zeros(num,1),VariableNames=["Model" "Jaccard" "Dice"]); % preallocate table

figure
mmTightTiledLayout

for n=1:num
    v = V(n,:); % get permutations
    mask1 = SegmentStrawberries(rgb, colorModels(v(1))); % extract mask1
    mask2 = SegmentStrawberries(rgb, colorModels(v(2))); % extract mask2

    nexttile % display thes
    imshowpair(mask1,mask2) % overlay masks
    title(join(colorModels(v),' ')); % title with the correct combination of color modes

    % add data to table
    t.Model(n) = join(colorModels(v),' '); % combines 2 string elements

    t.Jaccard(n) = jaccard(mask1,mask2); % calculate jaccard
    t.Dice(n) = dice(mask1, mask2); % calculate dice
end

end
%%
%[text] Call the compare function
%[text] - Green is mask1
%[text] - Magenta is mask2 \
t = compareMasks(p.rgb)
%[text] - how would you update the compareMasks function to use your strawberry segmentation function? \
%%
%[text] ## Segment Anything Model (SAM)
%[text] Ok, well that was fun. But what if you don't want to manually circle strawberries? Well, that's where [SAM](https://github.com/facebookresearch/segment-anything) comes into play. Brought to you by the same people who brought you Facebook and the Meta Glasses, SAM is a Machine Learning Model trained on 11 million images and 1.1 billion masked. It was designed to recognized objects in an image. Let's give it a whirl.
%[text] You can find SAM in the Image Segmenter App (not the colorThresholder
imageSegmenter(p.rgb)
%[text] - **Using Segment Anything Model (SAM)**:
%[text] -     Select the SAM option to segment the strawberries by clicking on the Strawberries (marking as "Foreground"). You will need to click on several strawberries
%[text] -     Remove any incorrect mask by marking that area as "Background \
%[text] 1. **Export the Code**: You can export the code used for segmentation by clicking on the appropriate option in the app. Review the code to get a sense of what is going on
%[text] 2. **Export the Mask** as maskSAM \
%%
%[text] ### Review SAM Mask
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,maskSAM)
%[text] ### 
%%
%[text] #### Clean mask
%[text] If there is any noise in the mask, you can remove with a morphological operation(s)
maskSAM = bwareaopen(maskSAM,50);
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,maskSAM)
%[text] - Notice, that some of the strawberries are touching \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9E120DDF] ### Separate Touching Blobs 
%[text] The watershed transform is used to detach connected components (CC) that are just barely touching. It's name comes from the geological concept of a watershed - a region of relatively high elevation separating two lower lying regions (or basin) 
%[text] ![](text:image:1318)![](text:image:0b66)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BA36DD21] #### mmGetWatershed
%[text] The course function **`mmGetWatershed`** automates the process of separating touching blobs. 
%[text] Here we use the following inputs:
%[text] 1. The mask
%[text] 2. The size of the regional minima (part of the watershed process)
%[text] 3. ShowSteps = true - visualizes the steps and the final outcome \
p.watershed = mmGetWatershed(maskSAM,5,"ShowSteps",true);
%[text] - and that works pretty good.
%[text] - What happens if you change Input 2 to a 1? \
%%
%[text] ### Review the final product
figure;
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,p.watershed);
%[text] - Pretty, pretty, good.  \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] # Back up Functions
function [BW] = SegmentStrawberries(RGB,colorModel)
% Converts Image to selected color model and then creates a mask based on
% the histogram slider settings for each channel

switch colorModel
    case 'rgb'
        % Convert RGB image to chosen color space
        I = RGB;

        % Define thresholds for channel 1 based on histogram settings
        channel1Min = 122.000;
        channel1Max = 255.000;

        % Define thresholds for channel 2 based on histogram settings
        channel2Min = 0.000;
        channel2Max = 130.000;

        % Define thresholds for channel 3 based on histogram settings
        channel3Min = 0.000;
        channel3Max = 168.000;

         % Create mask based on chosen histogram thresholds
        sliderBW = (I(:,:,1) >= channel1Min ) & (I(:,:,1) <= channel1Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,2) >= channel2Min ) & (I(:,:,2) <= channel2Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,3) >= channel3Min ) & (I(:,:,3) <= channel3Max);

    case 'hsv'
        % Convert RGB image to chosen color space
        I = rgb2hsv(RGB);

        % Define thresholds for channel 1 based on histogram settings
        channel1Min = 0.933;
        channel1Max = 0.048;

        % Define thresholds for channel 2 based on histogram settings
        channel2Min = 0.225;
        channel2Max = 1.000;

        % Define thresholds for channel 3 based on histogram settings
        channel3Min = 0.000;
        channel3Max = 1.000;

        % Create mask based on chosen histogram thresholds
        sliderBW = ( (I(:,:,1) >= channel1Min) | (I(:,:,1) <= channel1Max) ) & ...
            (I(:,:,2) >= channel2Min ) & (I(:,:,2) <= channel2Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,3) >= channel3Min ) & (I(:,:,3) <= channel3Max);
      
    case 'lab'
        % Convert RGB image to chosen color space
        I = rgb2lab(RGB);

        % Define thresholds for channel 1 based on histogram settings
        channel1Min = 20.206;
        channel1Max = 74.931;

        % Define thresholds for channel 2 based on histogram settings
        channel2Min = 16.586;
        channel2Max = 73.722;

        % Define thresholds for channel 3 based on histogram settings
        channel3Min = 8.345;
        channel3Max = 54.813;

        % Create mask based on chosen histogram thresholds
        sliderBW = (I(:,:,1) >= channel1Min ) & (I(:,:,1) <= channel1Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,2) >= channel2Min ) & (I(:,:,2) <= channel2Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,3) >= channel3Min ) & (I(:,:,3) <= channel3Max);
    otherwise
        disp('color model not found')
        return
end

BW = sliderBW;
BW = bwareaopen(BW,500); % remove noise
BW = imfill(BW,'holes');

end
%%
